1
00:00:06,200 --> 00:00:13,566
Recall Xavier initialization. A neuron with d_in standard normal inputs should output a standard normal too.

2
00:00:13,566 --> 00:00:20,566
With independent zero-mean weights, the expected square of h is the weight variance times the sum of squared inputs.

3
00:00:20,566 --> 00:00:28,633
If each squared input is about one, the sum is d_in, so the weight variance should be one over d_in.

4
00:00:28,633 --> 00:00:35,300
Feed random inputs through a layer. With unit-variance weights, outputs grow like the root of the fan-in.

5
00:00:35,300 --> 00:00:45,600
At d_in of 4096 that is about 64 times too large. With variance one over d_in, the size stays near one.

6
00:00:45,600 --> 00:00:52,966
Xavier quietly preserves a norm: the length divided by root d, the root-mean-square or RMS norm.

7
00:00:52,966 --> 00:01:00,333
Four entries of plus or minus one have length 2, sixteen have length 4. The RMS is one for both.

8
00:01:00,333 --> 00:01:05,200
RMS one means every entry is about size one.

9
00:01:05,200 --> 00:01:11,600
That is what the plot showed: with Xavier scaling, output RMS size matches input RMS size.

10
00:01:11,600 --> 00:01:18,966
Is there a matrix version of the RMS norm, measuring how much a weight matrix changes RMS size?

11
00:01:18,966 --> 00:01:27,366
Recall the induced matrix norm: the largest output size, measured in one norm, over inputs of size one in another.

12
00:01:27,366 --> 00:01:32,733
Use RMS on both sides: how much can A grow an input of RMS size one?

13
00:01:32,733 --> 00:01:39,500
An input of RMS one has length root d_in. An output's RMS is its length over root d_out.

14
00:01:39,500 --> 00:01:45,400
So the RMS to RMS norm is the spectral norm times root of d_in over d_out.

15
00:01:45,400 --> 00:01:55,966
Check: a 64 by 256 matrix has spectral norm 1.49, so its RMS norm is twice that, 2.98.

16
00:01:55,966 --> 00:02:03,700
Now put this norm into the optimizer recipe from episode one: steps of RMS to RMS size at most eta.

17
00:02:03,700 --> 00:02:11,800
The spectral size allowed is eta times root of d_out over d_in, so the best step is that factor times U V transpose.

18
00:02:11,800 --> 00:02:20,866
Try eta equal to 0.1 on three shapes. Wide-to-narrow layers get a smaller spectral step, narrow-to-wide a larger one.

19
00:02:20,866 --> 00:02:29,400
Yet each layer's output changes by exactly eta in RMS size. One number eta behaves like a layer-specific learning rate.

20
00:02:29,400 --> 00:02:38,600
Fan-in and fan-out do the adjusting. Making this precise for growing width is the idea behind maximal update parametrization.

